In article <8bs6mo$r0e$1@exnews.swisscom.com>,
 "Holger van Koll" <holger.vankoll@swisscom.com> writes:
> Yes, _this_ way (2 ifconfig-commands) it works; maybe I forgot to mention
> that in my first post.
> The problem is that you have to put it in one ifconfig-command (as there is
> only one in the default boot-scripts), and this is the problem.

Put the second ifconfig command in /etc/inet.local. From the comments
in /etc/inet.local:

# This script is called by /sbin/init.d/inet after all the
# network interfaces are up and running, but before any
# other network services have been started.
#
# Examples of things to put in here would be pfconfig,
# adding aliases for existing IP addresses, etc.
