Depends on the environment.
Where I work we have two computer rooms with about 400 meters between.
If a bomb goes off in one room, the computers in the other room should
take over automagically, and with the same up to date data. This means
that everything needs to be redundant, and data has to be in sync on
those redundant configurations.

Tru64 supports this setup very well, and that's why most of the machines
run Tru64.

You might say this setup is rare, but that's not the case in the world
of enterprise unix.

Regarding oldfashioned style. Yes, on Tru64 you need to do alot of stuff
the oldfashioned way, and I like it that way. The worst thing that can
happen on such a unix is that you'll actually learn something about
unix. If you spend every day in Smit, you'll never really get to know
what's happening behind the scene.

Ofcourse there's a marketing value in having good GUI tools and good
tools for starters, and that's why the old "Checklist" / Setup has been
enhanced on V5, where it's called SysMan and will do alot more.
Additionally there's a Insight Manager agent present in >= 4.0F, and you
can use the Windows program called StorageWorks Command Console to do
all RAID configuration & monitoring from remote.

And the Webes tools (
http://www1.service.digital.com/svctools/webes/index.html ) are also
getting pretty strong.

