In article <38BF8241.7D752A33@mfs.misys.co.uk>, Matt <matt@mfs.misys.co.uk> writes:

[ Since you figure out the core problem... ]

>My biggest annoyance is that mt is bloody useless.  On other platforms I
>can specify tape size, block size, media type, etc. using tape, et al.  

The tape length is irrelevent to the driver since it will write
till it gets to the end of the tape.  When that happens the driver
saves the status, so that software can check it and handle the
tape change as desired.  I forget which I/O control code is used
for this.  My recent experience suggests you'll also get an ENOSPC
errno value when the write fails.

For block size, do you mean transfer size or internal block size.
The transfer size is whatever is used by the application to write
the tape.  If the drive allows adjustments to its internal block
size, I think this can be done through the DDR database.  See the
ddr_config command and /etc/ddr.dbase file for more information.

If drives support multiple media types, the drive should be able
to figure that out for itself.  Density and compression are specified
based on the special file used.

>
>What does mt do on DU4.0d?  Unload and load tapes, marvellous!

Sometime in the V4 stream, the "status" sub-command was changed
to provide much more information than in previous versions.  Other
sub-commands allow:

o  Write End-of-File marks.
o  Skipping forward a specified number of files.
o  Skipping forward a specified number of records.
o  Skipping backward a specified number of files.
o  Skipping backward a specified number of records.
o  Bring an offline drive back online (assuming no
   physical interaction is required).
o  Space to the end of recorded data.

Naturally, the features will depend on whether the particular
actually implements the function.

The scu(8) command has its own built-in "mt" command, which may
support additional features.

Of the features you mentioned other mt commands providing, being
able to set the internal block size (on those drive where it is
supported), sounds like the most useful.

>Does anyone have any ideas about writing more than 8Gb of data to my
>DDS-3, or am I going about this completely the wrong way, and I am I
>wrong about the usefulness of mt on DU4.0d?

Don't use tapes longer than what the drive supports.  It is bad for
the drive and the tape.

