Dunno about the error, but are all of the cards seated properly?

To be fair, my CPQ field techs have almost always knew what to do [1]...

However, I question the company that assembled our AlphaServer 4100 (was
not CPQ). One time we called the field tech in to fix a tape-drive
problem. After he fixed the drive the system crashed and would not
reboot (all kinds of scrolling hardware errors on the console. Twasn't
pretty). 

After checking the usual external suspects, we finally opened up the
AlphaServer itself and found that one of the SCSI cards had not been
properly seated. It was actually sitting ON TOP of the screw that was
supposed to hold it down. Best guess, when the field tech buttoned up
the tape-drive and pushed it back into place, the stress on the cables
finally unseated the SCSI card the rest of the way. It was a crash
waiting to happen.

	[1] - Except for that one five years ago that accidently 
	changed the SCSI id on one of the tape drives, and spent 
	an hour trying to figure out why the system wouldn't 
	boot after it crashed. Of course, that's also how long it 
	took for me to notice the bad SCSI id.

