In article <8b14e8$hrr$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, Pascal Dorion <pdorion@latinmail.com> writes:
>
>How stable is the Alpha/Unix Platform?

Which Alpha system?  Which version of DEC OSF/1, Digital UNIX or
Tru64 UNIX?  What patches are installed?  What kind of crash?

Lots of things contribute to the stability or instability of
a system.  Without more information, it is to hard to guess
whether you're seeing something typical or not.  I'd guess
"not typical", but you haven't given me anything to base it
on.

As far as I know the 8400 is a stable system, though I've heard
that one has to be careful when plugging modules into the backplane
(the pins bend easily).  I've seen systems easily run for a year
or more with the only thing causing them crash is a power failure.

I've seen other systems where particular bugs were exercised on
a regular basis that crashed regularly until the bug was patched.

If the crashes are hardware related, all the parts *have* been
swapped and the system still crashes, then it may be that a
relevant part hasn't been swapped.  Or, some outside factor
is causing the system's instability.

