Pascal Dorion wrote:
> 
> Hi guys,
> 
> A quick question.
> 
> How stable is the Alpha/Unix Platform?
> We've got a 8400 with 10 CPUS and 12GB of RAM, most of it's parts have
> been changed in the passed 2 years yet it crashes at least once a month.
> It mainly runs Sybase under Digital Unix v4.0D. I'd like to hear some
> comments on stability with the Alpha platform in comparison with Sun
> and IBM RS/6000 computers.

We also have some 8400s with 8 CPUs (625 & 440 MHz) and 6 GB RAM and
unix 4.0D patch level 4. They run ASE cluster.
Primary applications are Oracle 7.3.4 and CA Unicenter TNG 2.1/2.2.

Even though they're the most busy machines we have, they're also the
most stable.

The only crashes we've seen lately (one last month, and then one back in
october 1999) has been CPU panics with the error message "free vnode
isn't".
We've seen this error message a couple of times on a ES40 cluster, and
common for the two platforms are CA Unicenter and Oracle.

Even though the error message points in the direction of something
filesystem related, we've checked this and it's not true.

We don't see the error on machines only running Oracle, and CA Unicenter
is the only application we have that build itself into the kernel and
hence is the only application capable of crashing a system this way.

In other words we think the crashes are caused by CA Unicenter. We've
logged a call to CA and they're looking at it.
Worth to notice is that we know another Alpha/dunix/Unicenter customer
had the same problem and recieved a fix from CA.

Without this software problem these machines could run for years without
a crash.

...so I find them very stable considering how much we run on them


So my questions are :

What patch level ?
What firmware level ?
What CPUs are you using ? (MHz and generation)
Did you analyse the crash data, and what was the result (hardware error
or software error) ?
What environment is your 8400 located in ?. (A clean room with a stable
temperature between 15 & 20 degrees celcius is where we keep ours
together with about 200 other Alpha, RS6000, Sparc & Intel servers).
Have you checked the binary.errlog and other log files to see if it's
constantly logging some kind of error ?
What are the primary warnings when you run a sys_check ?
Do you have a stable power supply ?
What kind of storage is connected to your machine, and at what firmware
level ?


Regarding comparison between Alpha, RS6000 and Sparc.
The two SP towers here have about the same uptime as the Alpha's. That's
mostly between 99.8 and 100% every month. But SPs are more expencive.
The Sparcs are few and don't run critical stuff here so I don't know
anything exact about them, but I do know that the really big Sparc boxes
have had problems with hardware stability when you expand them with lots
of CPUs & memory (something you need to get the performance of a 10 CPU
AS8400 or better).
Another issue is that a new Alpha with EV6(.7) CPUs outperform the most,
if not all.
Worth to notice is also that a 4 CPU ES40 will outperform a 10 CPU
AS8400.
If you have single threaded jobs that take alot of CPU time, then go for
the fastest CPU, and this is surely not the Sparc.



l8r/Jspr



