If you are looking for High Availability, yes it does.  Especially for
the 8400s since they can get real complex which slows down booting.  If
a swap partition is on a physical disk that dies then potientially the
OS can crash, or applications, not always but it depends on various
factors.  Thus HA systems usually have the swap partitions mirrored
along with / and /usr.


> > First of all, can I  place all swap on a drive which is
> > not root drive or should I have some small amount of swap on root
drive?
>
> Not required.  I've done this (no swap on root drive) before.  Booting
> into
> single user mode "complains" but works fine (especially on a system
with
> 8GB of memory !!!)
>

You do not need to have any swap space on the root drive.  Although by
default it looks there.  Remember to change /etc/fstab and
/sbin/swapdefault appropriately.  Make sure to decide on which mode of
swap utilization you want.  There are two: immediate mode {default} and
deferred (over-commit) mode.  In immediate mode, when a process is
started, the system tries to reserve swap equal to the process size, if
there is not enough the process is not allowed to start.  In deferred
mode, the system allocates space when a process needs to be swapped,
thus you can get away with less.  If you are looking for performance try
having multiple swap partitions on different drives.  Digital Unix
(Tru64) allocates swap in a round robin fashion.  Depending on your swap
mode and the processes running on your system 1 to 2 times the physical
memory is generally enough when you start talking about GBs,although
some types of processes can upset this.

> > Or should I be putting in the swap drives on the other empty cabinet
for
> > better performance?(for data we have an ESA 10000 and hence we don't
use
> > the built in cabinets on the 8400 for anything other than root
drives)

Depends, is this cabinet an extension of the ESA 10000, or are they just
disk shelves off of different controllers? For performance you could
also consider mirroring&striping. There are many factors to consider
when looking at this.

