In article <38BA9D45.CE9F53A2@mfs.misys.co.uk>, Matt <matt@mfs.misys.co.uk> writes:

>I grasp that shifting larger blocks is going to be quicker, but the
>performance difference is staggering.  On SCO or Linux altering the
>block size does not make this kind of difference.

All versions of DEC OSF/1, Digital UNIX and Tru64 UNIX, should
support using transfer sizes as large SCSI-2 allows.  I'm fairly
certain that modern versions do.  Based on a little testing I've
done with large transfer sizes, the one that is "too large" is
the one that causes the system to start paging and swapping.

Some HS family array controllers support a limited transfer
size (64 KB) at the controller.  If you think you may have
to restore data on a tape drive connected to such a data,
use a 64 KB transfer size.  Some tapes do prefer fairly large
block sizes, so their utility on such subsystems is limited.
I think this limitation was lifted on the HSZ70 and later
controllers, but I haven't checked closely.

One affect this limitation has, is that recent versions of
tar use an initial large transfer size to "guess" the block
size at which the tape was written.  I think it uses 1 MB.
On tapes connected to these HS subsystems, this causes an
I/O error because the controller rejects the command.  The
solution is to use the explicit block size if it is one the
controller supports.

