In article <38bd2f7e.1116842927@192.9.200.16>, john.allen@geonet-tech.com (John Allen) writes:

>While doing vdumps on mounted drives, we occasionally get "I/O Error 5
>reading/writing from/to drive".  This condition then hangs the system
>for a considerable amount of time.  I kill the process, and manually
>dismount the tape.

I/O errors are generally device errors.  Check the error log to see
if there really was one and if so, get the device fixed.  Tape drivers
have to be especially careful when recovering from errors since tapes
are sequential devices.  Disks only have to abort the command, seek
back to the desired place and rewrite the data.  Tapes can't seek to
the block level.  The driver's error handling is probably what hung,
waiting for the tape to complete a command (that it may not have
been able to complete).  Shipping the drive or the bus a reset may
get things unstuck, but you'll want to rewind the tape (if the reset
doesn't) and start over.

There have been driver bugs (not vdump bugs) that cause what look
like I/O errors to application, but which aren't device errors.
Check Customer Services for patches if you don't find any device
errors.  One relatively common error occured on TZ88s connected
to KZPAA class controllers when using vdump.  This class of
adapters were the built-in adapters of the AlphaServer 2100s
and comparible systems.  The only solution I ever saw for that
was replace the KZPAA with a KZPDA.

>
>Does anybody have any knowledge of this as a bug in vdump?  And, if
>so, is there a patch which fixes it?
